1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,200
One neuron is y = mx + b, with one switch on the end.

2
00:00:03,200 --> 00:00:05,600
One neuron. That is the whole thing.

3
00:00:05,600 --> 00:00:07,200
x is what comes in.

4
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:08,800
w is how much it matters.

5
00:00:08,800 --> 00:00:10,400
b is how hard it is to set off.

6
00:00:10,400 --> 00:00:13,600
Put real numbers on it. The weight is 2.

7
00:00:13,600 --> 00:00:16,000
What comes in is 3.

8
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:18,400
The bias is minus 4 — the input has to beat 4.

9
00:00:18,400 --> 00:00:21,600
Two times three is six. Six minus four is two.

10
00:00:21,600 --> 00:00:25,600
Positive, so it goes straight through. The neuron fires. Output 2.

11
00:00:25,600 --> 00:00:28,800
Same neuron. Change only what comes in — now it is 1.

12
00:00:28,800 --> 00:00:32,800
Two times one is two. Two minus four is minus two. Negative, so nothing gets through. Same neuron, silent.

13
00:00:32,800 --> 00:00:36,400
We learned this at school. Nobody ever said what for.

14
00:00:36,400 --> 00:00:40,000
Pause. Observe. Learn. Follow for the math behind AI — @observer.collapse
